WEBVTT
NOTE OpenIE Research TV v3.0 — spellcheck (180s) Narrator Misha

1
00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.442
First, people once paid hundreds of dollars for standalone spell check,

2
00:00:05.442 --> 00:00:07.947
yet today it comes free everywhere.

3
00:00:08.507 --> 00:00:12.056
Second, it became free because checking a word costs

4
00:00:12.056 --> 00:00:15.537
almost nothing to run inside tools you already use.

5
00:00:16.097 --> 00:00:20.745
Third, new computer intelligence reaches seven billion people only

6
00:00:20.745 --> 00:00:24.337
when it runs cheaply on devices people already own.

7
00:00:24.897 --> 00:00:32.659
Even after frontier chat crossed eight hundred million weekly users, scarcity pricing still holds capabilities inside

8
00:00:32.659 --> 00:00:38.431
rented data centers and sells monthly access to the few who can pay. History shows that

9
00:00:38.431 --> 00:00:46.657
computer skills reach more than seven billion people only when separate per-seat and per-word invoices disappear completely.

10
00:00:47.217 --> 00:00:54.054
Open a January 1981 computer magazine and you find WordCheck for dual-disk personal computers advertised at

11
00:00:54.054 --> 00:01:01.275
a list price of two hundred dollars, followed by eighty-nine-dollar and ninety-nine-dollar grammar add-ons in the

12
00:01:01.275 --> 00:01:09.198
late 1980s. Back then, proofreading software was a premium standalone purchase, just like early electronic desk calculators.

13
00:01:09.758 --> 00:01:16.862
Spell check vanished as a separate product because engineers deliberately shrunk the procedure onto the machine that

14
00:01:16.862 --> 00:01:24.518
already held the document. Prefix stripping and hashing packed thirty thousand English words into twenty-six thousand machine

15
00:01:24.518 --> 00:01:32.296
words in 1982, and open-source morphological checkers later shipped free inside browsers, office suites, and operating systems.

16
00:01:32.856 --> 00:01:38.512
Economics explains why the separate price fell all the way to zero. Checking a word is most

17
00:01:38.512 --> 00:01:45.348
useful right beside the cursor where you are already writing, and once local dictionary lookup plus one-letter

18
00:01:45.348 --> 00:01:51.812
edit formulas cost almost zero energy to run, metering each check made less sense than bundling it free.

19
00:01:52.372 --> 00:01:59.954
Our three-dimensional global cadence model contrasts centralized high-wattage server hubs against the installed fleet people

20
00:01:59.954 --> 00:02:06.619
already own—five point eight billion mobile subscribers worldwide, over two point three billion active phones

21
00:02:06.619 --> 00:02:13.283
and tablets in one ecosystem alone, and more than three hundred ten billion cumulative microcontroller chips.

22
00:02:13.843 --> 00:02:20.630
A capability completes the automation path when five strict conditions hold: the work sits on ordinary

23
00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:27.018
hardware already in hand, runs as a local routine rather than a remote bureau job, fits everyday

24
00:02:27.018 --> 00:02:35.403
memory and battery envelopes, requires no frontier data-center rental, and returns an accurate answer without cutting corners.

25
00:02:35.963 --> 00:02:42.366
Every single figure on our price tape cites its exact dated advertisement, museum brochure, or release

26
00:02:42.366 --> 00:02:49.084
note, leaving unprinted months blank instead of inventing smooth curves. Nor do we guess energy numbers for

27
00:02:49.084 --> 00:02:57.057
closed phone keyboards—the documented historical transition from two hundred dollars to bundled zero speaks clearly for itself.
